select public.fd_ai_upsert_chunks('fd-textbook', '1b1a21163698', 8, $aim_json$[{"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:8.0#1", "source_key": "fd-textbook:8.0", "chapter": 8, "section": "8.0", "heading": "Overview", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 1, "title": "Chapter 8, Option Markets and the Properties of Option Prices — 8.0 Overview", "body": "Bicksler, J., and Chen, A. H. (1986). An economic analysis of interest rate swaps. Journal of Finance, 41(3), 645–655.\nHull, J. C. (2022). Options, Futures, and Other Derivatives (11th ed.). Pearson. Chapter 7.\nLitzenberger, R. H. (1992). Swaps: plain and fanciful. Journal of Finance, 47(3), 831–850.\nSmith, C. W., Smithson, C. W., and Wakeman, L. M. (1988). The market for interest rate swaps. Financial Management, 17(4), 34–44.\nTurnbull, S. M. (1987). Swaps: a zero sum game? Financial Management, 16(1), 15–21."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:8.0#2", "source_key": "fd-textbook:8.0", "chapter": 8, "section": "8.0", "heading": "Decide first", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 2, "title": "Chapter 8, Option Markets and the Properties of Option Prices — 8.0 Decide first", "body": "⚙ Decision — choose first; the theory follows\nA call option is offered at a low price. Is there a certain profit?\nNorthwind shares trade at $28.00 and will pay no dividend in the next six months. A European call with a strike price of $25.00 and six months to expiration is quoted at the price shown. Money can be borrowed or invested at 5 percent per year with continuous compounding. No model of the share price is available, and none is needed. Set the quote, select a strategy, and examine the result; then find the lowest quote at which no certain profit exists.\nA call with a strike of $25 on a share worth $28 is worth at least $3: so much is evident. The exercise shows that it is worth at least $3.62, because the $25 need not be paid for six months. This chapter establishes what can be said about option prices from arbitrage alone, before any assumption is made about how share prices behave. The results hold in every model."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:8.1#3", "source_key": "fd-textbook:8.1", "chapter": 8, "section": "8.1", "heading": "Option markets", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 3, "title": "Chapter 8, Option Markets and the Properties of Option Prices — 8.1 Option markets", "body": "Chapter 1 defined calls and puts, European and American exercise, the strike price, the expiration date, and the positions of buyer and writer. This section describes how options on shares are traded on exchanges. The largest markets are in the United States, where options on several thousand shares, on indices, and on exchange-traded funds are listed on exchanges including the Chicago Board Options Exchange; Eurex in Frankfurt and the exchanges of Asia operate on similar principles."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:8.1#4", "source_key": "fd-textbook:8.1", "chapter": 8, "section": "8.1", "heading": "Specification", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 4, "title": "Chapter 8, Option Markets and the Properties of Option Prices — 8.1 Specification", "body": "A listed option on a share is ordinarily American and covers 100 shares. Prices are quoted per share, so that a quoted premium of $1.50 means a payment of $150 for one contract. The exchange specifies the following.\nExpiration dates. Standard options expire on the third Friday of the expiration month. Options are listed with expirations in the nearest months and in more distant months according to a fixed cycle; for actively traded shares there are also weekly expirations and long-dated options with up to about three years to run.\nStrike prices. Strikes are set at regular intervals, typically $1, $2.50, or $5 according to the share price, on both sides of the current price. New strikes are added as the share price moves. All options of the same type (call or put) on a share form an option class; all options in a class with the same strike and expiration form an option series.\nMoneyness. Section 1.5 defined an option as in the money, at the money, or out of the money according to whether immediate exercise would give a positive, zero, or negative payoff. The intrinsic value is \\(\\max(S-K,0)\\) for a call and \\(\\max(K-S,0)\\) for a put, and the remainder of the price is the time value."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:8.1#5", "source_key": "fd-textbook:8.1", "chapter": 8, "section": "8.1", "heading": "Dividends and stock splits", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 5, "title": "Chapter 8, Option Markets and the Properties of Option Prices — 8.1 Dividends and stock splits", "body": "Listed options are not adjusted for ordinary cash dividends. When a share goes ex-dividend, that is, begins to trade without the right to a dividend just declared, its price falls by approximately the amount of the dividend, and the terms of the option are unchanged. A dividend therefore reduces the value of a call and raises the value of a put.\nOptions are adjusted for stock splits and stock dividends. In an \\(n\\)-for-\\(m\\) split, the strike price is multiplied by \\(m/n\\) and the number of shares covered by \\(n/m\\). After a 2-for-1 split, a contract on 100 shares with a strike of $60 becomes a contract on 200 shares with a strike of $30, which leaves the positions of the buyer and the writer unchanged."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:8.1#6", "source_key": "fd-textbook:8.1", "chapter": 8, "section": "8.1", "heading": "Trading, clearing, and margin", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 6, "title": "Chapter 8, Option Markets and the Properties of Option Prices — 8.1 Trading, clearing, and margin", "body": "Most exchanges rely on market makers, who quote both a bid price, at which they will buy, and a higher ask price (or offer price), at which they will sell. The difference, the bid–ask spread, is their compensation and is a cost to other traders. Exchanges limit its size.\nIn the United States all listed options are cleared by the Options Clearing Corporation (OCC), which performs the function described in Section 2.4: it stands between buyer and writer and guarantees performance. When a holder exercises, the OCC assigns the exercise to a member with a short position in the same series, and the member assigns it to a client.\nThe buyer of an option pays the premium in full and has no further obligation. The writer has a potential liability and must post margin. A writer who does not hold an offsetting position in the share has written a naked option and must post margin equal to the premium received plus a percentage of the value of the underlying shares, adjusted for the amount by which the option is out of the money. A writer of a call who owns the shares has written a covered call and need post no further margin, since the shares can be delivered if the call is exercised."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:8.1#7", "source_key": "fd-textbook:8.1", "chapter": 8, "section": "8.1", "heading": "Other options", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 7, "title": "Chapter 8, Option Markets and the Properties of Option Prices — 8.1 Other options", "body": "Options on stock indices are settled in cash and are usually European. Large institutions trade options over the counter with terms of their own choosing. Three instruments issued by companies themselves resemble call options: warrants, which are calls issued by a company on its own shares; employee stock options, granted to staff as part of their remuneration; and convertible bonds, which may be exchanged for shares at a specified ratio. Unlike listed options, their exercise leads the company to issue new shares."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:8.2#8", "source_key": "fd-textbook:8.2", "chapter": 8, "section": "8.2", "heading": "Factors affecting option prices", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 8, "title": "Chapter 8, Option Markets and the Properties of Option Prices — 8.2 Factors affecting option prices", "body": "Six factors determine the price of an option on a share: the current share price \\(S_0\\); the strike price \\(K\\); the time to expiration \\(T\\); the volatility of the share price, \\(\\sigma\\); the risk-free interest rate \\(r\\); and the dividends expected during the life of the option. Table 8.1 gives the effect on the option price of an increase in each factor with the others held fixed.\nTable 8.1 Effect on the price of an option of an increase in one variable, the others unchanged\nVariableEuropean callEuropean putAmerican callAmerican put\nShare priceRisesFallsRisesFalls\nStrike priceFallsRisesFallsRises\nTime to expirationUncertainUncertainRisesRises\nVolatilityRisesRisesRisesRises\nRisk-free rateRisesFallsRisesFalls\nDividendsFallsRisesFallsRises\nShare price and strike price. A call pays the excess of the share price over the strike; it is worth more the higher the share price and the lower the strike. A put pays the excess of the strike over the share price, and the effects are reversed."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:8.2#9", "source_key": "fd-textbook:8.2", "chapter": 8, "section": "8.2", "heading": "Factors affecting option prices", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 9, "title": "Chapter 8, Option Markets and the Properties of Option Prices — 8.2 Factors affecting option prices", "body": "Time to expiration. The holder of an American option with a long life has every opportunity open to the holder of one with a short life, and more; the longer option cannot be worth less. For European options this need not hold. A European call that expires just before a large dividend may be worth more than one that expires just after it, and a deep in-the-money European put may be worth more with a short life, because the strike price is received sooner.\nVolatility. The volatility of a share price, denoted \\(\\sigma\\), is a measure of the uncertainty about its future movements; it is defined precisely in Chapter 12 as the standard deviation of the return on the share over one year, when the return is measured with continuous compounding. As volatility increases, very high and very low share prices both become more likely. The owner of a share is exposed to both and the effects offset. The owner of an option is not: the holder of a call gains from high prices and loses no more than the premium from low prices, and the holder of a put gains from low prices. Higher volatility therefore raises the value of both."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:8.2#10", "source_key": "fd-textbook:8.2", "chapter": 8, "section": "8.2", "heading": "Factors affecting option prices", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 10, "title": "Chapter 8, Option Markets and the Properties of Option Prices — 8.2 Factors affecting option prices", "body": "Risk-free rate. A higher interest rate lowers the present value of the strike price. The holder of a call will pay the strike and gains; the holder of a put will receive it and loses. The statement assumes that the share price itself is unchanged, although in practice a rise in rates is often accompanied by a fall in share prices.\nDividends. A dividend lowers the share price on the ex-dividend date, which reduces the value of a call and raises the value of a put.\nConcept check 8.1\nA company announces results that leave its share price unchanged but make its future prospects considerably more uncertain. What happens to the prices of calls and puts on its shares?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:8.3#11", "source_key": "fd-textbook:8.3", "chapter": 8, "section": "8.3", "heading": "Upper and lower bounds", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 11, "title": "Chapter 8, Option Markets and the Properties of Option Prices — 8.3 Upper and lower bounds", "body": "The following notation is used: \\(c\\) and \\(p\\) are the prices of a European call and a European put, and \\(C\\) and \\(P\\) the prices of the corresponding American options, all with strike \\(K\\) and time to expiration \\(T\\) on a share with current price \\(S_0\\) and price \\(S_T\\) at expiration. Dividends are assumed to be zero until Section 8.6. The assumptions are those of Section 4.1: no transaction costs, borrowing and lending at the risk-free rate \\(r\\), and the prompt removal of arbitrage opportunities."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:8.3#12", "source_key": "fd-textbook:8.3", "chapter": 8, "section": "8.3", "heading": "Upper bounds", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 12, "title": "Chapter 8, Option Markets and the Properties of Option Prices — 8.3 Upper bounds", "body": "A call gives the right to buy one share, and cannot be worth more than the share: \\(c\\le S_0\\) and \\(C\\le S_0\\). If it were, an arbitrageur would buy the share and write the call. An American put gives the right to sell for \\(K\\), and cannot be worth more than \\(K\\): \\(P\\le K\\). A European put will be worth at most \\(K\\) at expiration, and so at most its present value today: \\(p\\le Ke^{-rT}\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:8.3#13", "source_key": "fd-textbook:8.3", "chapter": 8, "section": "8.3", "heading": "Lower bound for a European call", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 13, "title": "Chapter 8, Option Markets and the Properties of Option Prices — 8.3 Lower bound for a European call", "body": "Result 8.1 — Lower bounds for European options on a share paying no dividend\n\\[ c\\ \\ge\\ \\max\\!\\left(S_0-Ke^{-rT},\\,0\\right), \\qquad p\\ \\ge\\ \\max\\!\\left(Ke^{-rT}-S_0,\\,0\\right). \\]\nProof for the call. Compare two portfolios. Portfolio A consists of one European call and a zero-coupon bond that pays \\(K\\) at time \\(T\\); the bond costs \\(Ke^{-rT}\\) today. Portfolio B consists of one share. At time \\(T\\), if \\(S_T>K\\), the call is exercised using the proceeds of the bond, and portfolio A is worth \\(S_T\\); if \\(S_T\\le K\\), the call expires and portfolio A is worth \\(K\\). Portfolio A is therefore worth \\(\\max(S_T,K)\\), which is never less than the value \\(S_T\\) of portfolio B. A portfolio that is worth at least as much as another in every outcome cannot cost less today, so \\(c+Ke^{-rT}\\ge S_0\\). Since a call cannot have negative value, \\(c\\ge\\max(S_0-Ke^{-rT},0)\\). \\(\\blacksquare\\)\nProof for the put. Portfolio C consists of one European put and one share; portfolio D of a zero-coupon bond paying \\(K\\) at \\(T\\). At time \\(T\\) portfolio C is worth \\(\\max(S_T,K)\\), which is never less than \\(K\\). Hence \\(p+S_0\\ge Ke^{-rT}\\). \\(\\blacksquare\\)"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:8.3#14", "source_key": "fd-textbook:8.3", "chapter": 8, "section": "8.3", "heading": "Lower bound for a European call", "kind": "example", "ord": 14, "title": "Chapter 8, Option Markets and the Properties of Option Prices — 8.3 Lower bound for a European call", "body": "Worked example 8.1 — A violated lower bound\nIn the opening exercise, \\(S_0=28\\), \\(K=25\\), \\(r=5\\) percent, and \\(T=0.5\\), and the call is quoted at $3.20. Verify that the bound is violated and set out the arbitrage.\n1\nThe bound. \\(Ke^{-rT}=25e^{-0.025}=\\$24.38\\). Lower bound: \\(28-24.38=\\$3.62\\). The quote of $3.20 is below it.\n2\nToday. Buy the call for $3.20 and sell the share short for $28.00: net receipt $24.80. Invest $24.38 for six months. Cash remaining: \\(24.80-24.38=\\$0.42\\).\n3\nAt expiration, \\(S_T>25\\). The investment has grown to $25.00. Exercise the call, pay $25.00, and return the share. Net cash flow: zero.\n4\nAt expiration, \\(S_T\\le25\\). Buy the share in the market for \\(S_T\\) and return it. Net cash flow: \\(25-S_T\\ge0\\).\n5\nConclusion. The arbitrageur receives $0.42 per share today and cannot owe anything at expiration. Purchases of the call raise its price until the bound is restored."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:8.3#15", "source_key": "fd-textbook:8.3", "chapter": 8, "section": "8.3", "heading": "Lower bound for a European call", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 15, "title": "Chapter 8, Option Markets and the Properties of Option Prices — 8.3 Lower bound for a European call", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 8.1 — Bounds on option prices\nThe price of an option lies between two limits set by arbitrage alone\nThe dashed lines are the upper and lower bounds for a European option with a strike of $30. The solid line is the value given by the Black–Scholes–Merton model of Chapter 12, shown here only to locate the price between the bounds. Vary the time to expiration, the interest rate, and the volatility. The bounds do not depend on volatility; the price does."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:8.4#16", "source_key": "fd-textbook:8.4", "chapter": 8, "section": "8.4", "heading": "Put–call parity", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 16, "title": "Chapter 8, Option Markets and the Properties of Option Prices — 8.4 Put–call parity", "body": "The two portfolios used in the proofs of Result 8.1 lead to the most important relation of this chapter. Portfolio A (a European call and a zero-coupon bond paying \\(K\\)) and portfolio C (a European put and a share) are each worth \\(\\max(S_T,K)\\) at expiration. The options are European and cannot be exercised earlier. Two portfolios with identical values at \\(T\\) and no intermediate cash flows must have identical values today.\nResult 8.2 — Put–call parity\nFor European options with the same strike price and expiration date on a share that pays no dividend, \\[ c+Ke^{-rT}=p+S_0 . \\]\nTable 8.2 Values of the two portfolios at expiration\nPortfolioComponent\\(S_T>K\\)\\(S_T\\le K\\)\nACall\\(S_T-K\\)0\nZero-coupon bond\\(K\\)\\(K\\)\nTotal\\(S_T\\)\\(K\\)\nCPut0\\(K-S_T\\)\nShare\\(S_T\\)\\(S_T\\)\nTotal\\(S_T\\)\\(K\\)"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:8.4#17", "source_key": "fd-textbook:8.4", "chapter": 8, "section": "8.4", "heading": "Put–call parity", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 17, "title": "Chapter 8, Option Markets and the Properties of Option Prices — 8.4 Put–call parity", "body": "Parity has three uses. It gives the price of a put from the price of a call with the same terms, or the reverse, so that only one of the two need be valued. It shows how any one of the four instruments — call, put, share, and bond — can be constructed from the other three; rearranged as \\(c=p+S_0-Ke^{-rT}\\), it states that a call is equivalent to a put together with a share financed in part by borrowing. And when market prices depart from it, it identifies an arbitrage."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:8.4#18", "source_key": "fd-textbook:8.4", "chapter": 8, "section": "8.4", "heading": "Put–call parity", "kind": "example", "ord": 18, "title": "Chapter 8, Option Markets and the Properties of Option Prices — 8.4 Put–call parity", "body": "Worked example 8.2 — Parity and its violation\nNorthwind shares trade at $28. A three-month European call with a strike of $30 costs $1.50, and the risk-free rate is 5 percent. (a) Find the price of the European put with the same terms. (b) The put is quoted at $2.60. Set out the arbitrage.\n1\n(a) \\(Ke^{-rT}=30e^{-0.0125}=\\$29.63\\). \\(p=c+Ke^{-rT}-S_0=1.50+29.63-28.00=\\$3.13\\).\n2\n(b) Diagnosis. At $2.60 the put is cheap relative to the call: \\(p+S_0=30.60\\), whereas \\(c+Ke^{-rT}=31.13\\). Buy the portfolio that is cheap and sell the one that is dear.\n3\nToday. Buy the put for $2.60 and the share for $28.00; write the call for $1.50. Net cost: \\(2.60+28.00-1.50=\\$29.10\\), which is borrowed for three months.\n4\nAt expiration. If \\(S_T>30\\), the call is exercised against the arbitrageur, who delivers the share for $30. If \\(S_T\\le30\\), the arbitrageur exercises the put and sells the share for $30. In either case $30.00 is received.\n5\nProfit. Repay \\(29.10e^{0.0125}=\\$29.47\\). Riskless profit: \\(30.00-29.47=\\$0.53\\) per share."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:8.4#19", "source_key": "fd-textbook:8.4", "chapter": 8, "section": "8.4", "heading": "Put–call parity", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 19, "title": "Chapter 8, Option Markets and the Properties of Option Prices — 8.4 Put–call parity", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 8.2 — Put–call parity\nAny departure from parity is an arbitrage\nSet the quoted prices of the three-month call and put with a strike of $30. The table compares the two portfolios, and the text sets out the trade and its profit. Find a pair of prices at which no profit remains."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:8.4#20", "source_key": "fd-textbook:8.4", "chapter": 8, "section": "8.4", "heading": "Put–call parity", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 20, "title": "Chapter 8, Option Markets and the Properties of Option Prices — 8.4 Put–call parity", "body": "Concept check 8.2\nA share trades at $50 and pays no dividend. A one-year European put with a strike of $50 costs $3.00, and the risk-free rate is 4 percent with continuous compounding. What is the price of the one-year European call with the same strike?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:8.5#21", "source_key": "fd-textbook:8.5", "chapter": 8, "section": "8.5", "heading": "Calls on a share that pays no dividend", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 21, "title": "Chapter 8, Option Markets and the Properties of Option Prices — 8.5 Calls on a share that pays no dividend", "body": "Result 8.3\nIt is never optimal to exercise an American call on a share that pays no dividend before the expiration date. Consequently \\(C=c\\).\nAn American call is worth at least as much as the European call, and by Result 8.1 the European call is worth at least \\(S_0-Ke^{-rT}\\). When \\(r>0\\) and \\(T>0\\) this exceeds \\(S_0-K\\), the amount obtained by exercising. A holder who no longer wishes to own the option does better to sell it than to exercise it.\nTwo economic reasons lie behind the result. First, exercise means paying the strike price now instead of at expiration, which forgoes interest on \\(K\\). Second, exercise gives up the protection that the option provides: after exercise the holder owns the share and bears the whole of any fall in its price, whereas the holder of the call can lose no more than its value. Even a holder who is certain that the share is overvalued should sell the call, or sell the share short against it, and should not exercise."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:8.5#22", "source_key": "fd-textbook:8.5", "chapter": 8, "section": "8.5", "heading": "Puts", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 22, "title": "Chapter 8, Option Markets and the Properties of Option Prices — 8.5 Puts", "body": "For a put the argument does not hold. Exercising a put means receiving the strike price now, and interest can be earned on it. Consider the extreme case of a share whose price has fallen almost to zero. Exercise yields almost \\(K\\) at once. Waiting can add almost nothing, since the share price cannot fall below zero, and it delays the receipt of \\(K\\). Exercise is optimal. More generally, an American put should be exercised early whenever the share price is sufficiently far below the strike. It follows that an American put is worth more than the corresponding European put, \\(P>p\\), and that a European put can be worth less than its intrinsic value, as Interactive figure 8.1 shows.\nThe share price below which exercise is optimal depends on the interest rate, the volatility, and the time remaining, and has no simple formula. Chapter 10 finds it numerically, and Interactive figure 10.3 displays it."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:8.5#23", "source_key": "fd-textbook:8.5", "chapter": 8, "section": "8.5", "heading": "Puts", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 23, "title": "Chapter 8, Option Markets and the Properties of Option Prices — 8.5 Puts", "body": "★ Optional: the relation between American call and put prices. Put–call parity is an equality only for European options. For American options on a share that pays no dividend, \\[ S_0-K\\ \\le\\ C-P\\ \\le\\ S_0-Ke^{-rT}. \\] Proof of the right-hand inequality. \\(C=c\\) and \\(P\\ge p\\), so \\(C-P\\le c-p=S_0-Ke^{-rT}\\). Proof of the left-hand inequality. Compare portfolio E, a European call and cash of \\(K\\) invested at the rate \\(r\\), with portfolio F, an American put and a share. If the put is not exercised early, F is worth \\(\\max(S_T,K)\\) at \\(T\\) and E is worth \\(\\max(S_T,K)+Ke^{rT}-K\\), which is larger. If the put is exercised at an earlier time \\(\\tau\\), F is worth \\(K\\) at that time, while E is worth at least the invested cash, \\(Ke^{r\\tau}\\ge K\\). In every case E is worth at least as much as F, so \\(c+K\\ge P+S_0\\); with \\(c=C\\) this is \\(C-P\\ge S_0-K\\). \\(\\blacksquare\\)"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:8.6#24", "source_key": "fd-textbook:8.6", "chapter": 8, "section": "8.6", "heading": "The effect of dividends", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 24, "title": "Chapter 8, Option Markets and the Properties of Option Prices — 8.6 The effect of dividends", "body": "Let \\(D\\) denote the present value of the dividends that will be paid during the life of the option; for listed options, whose lives are short, these can be predicted with reasonable accuracy. A share that will pay dividends is worth \\(D\\) more than the claim to the share at time \\(T\\) alone. Replacing portfolio B of Section 8.3 by a share and portfolio A by a call together with cash of \\(D+Ke^{-rT}\\) gives the modified results:\n\\[ c\\ge S_0-D-Ke^{-rT},\\qquad p\\ge D+Ke^{-rT}-S_0,\\qquad c+D+Ke^{-rT}=p+S_0 . \\]\nWhen dividends are expected, Result 8.3 no longer holds. It can be optimal to exercise an American call immediately before an ex-dividend date, because the holder of the share receives the dividend and the holder of the call does not, and the share price will fall when the dividend is paid. Early exercise of a call is worth considering only at those dates, and only when the dividend is large relative to the interest forgone on the strike."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:8.6#25", "source_key": "fd-textbook:8.6", "chapter": 8, "section": "8.6", "heading": "The effect of dividends", "kind": "example", "ord": 25, "title": "Chapter 8, Option Markets and the Properties of Option Prices — 8.6 The effect of dividends", "body": "Worked example 8.3 — Parity with a dividend\nA share trades at $28 and will pay a dividend of $0.40 in three months. A six-month European call with a strike of $28 costs $3.10. The risk-free rate is 5 percent for all maturities. Find the price of the six-month European put with the same strike.\n1\nPresent value of the dividend. \\(D=0.40e^{-0.05\\times0.25}=\\$0.395\\).\n2\nPresent value of the strike. \\(28e^{-0.05\\times0.5}=\\$27.309\\).\n3\nPut. \\(p=c+D+Ke^{-rT}-S_0=3.10+0.395+27.309-28.00=\\$2.80\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:8.6#26", "source_key": "fd-textbook:8.6", "chapter": 8, "section": "8.6", "heading": "The effect of dividends", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 26, "title": "Chapter 8, Option Markets and the Properties of Option Prices — 8.6 The effect of dividends", "body": "Concept check 8.3\nAn investor holds an American call, deep in the money, on a share that pays no dividend, and is convinced that the share price is about to fall. What should the investor do?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:8.6#27", "source_key": "fd-textbook:8.6", "chapter": 8, "section": "8.6", "heading": "The effect of dividends", "kind": "notes", "ord": 27, "title": "Chapter 8, Option Markets and the Properties of Option Prices — 8.6 The effect of dividends", "body": "Historical note\nPut–call parity was known to option dealers long before it appeared in the academic literature. Russell Sage, a New York financier of the 1870s, used the relation to construct loans from options and shares at rates above those permitted by the usury laws of the time, and a description of the conversion of puts into calls appears in a handbook for traders published in London in 1902. The first formal statement in a journal is that of Hans Stoll in 1969.\nThe systematic derivation of bounds on option prices from the absence of arbitrage, without any assumption about the distribution of share prices, is due to Robert Merton, whose paper of 1973 established most of the results of this chapter, including the proposition that an American call on a share paying no dividend should not be exercised early."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:8.summary#28", "source_key": "fd-textbook:8.summary", "chapter": 8, "section": "8.summary", "heading": "Summary", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 28, "title": "Chapter 8, Option Markets and the Properties of Option Prices — 8.summary Summary", "body": "Listed options on shares are American, cover 100 shares, expire on standard dates, and are adjusted for stock splits but not for cash dividends. Buyers pay the premium in full; writers post margin.\nThe price of an option depends on the share price, the strike, the time to expiration, the volatility, the risk-free rate, and dividends. Volatility raises the price of both calls and puts.\nArbitrage alone implies \\(\\max(S_0-Ke^{-rT},0)\\le c\\le S_0\\) and \\(\\max(Ke^{-rT}-S_0,0)\\le p\\le Ke^{-rT}\\).\nPut–call parity, \\(c+Ke^{-rT}=p+S_0\\), links the prices of European calls and puts with the same terms; with dividends of present value \\(D\\), \\(c+D+Ke^{-rT}=p+S_0\\).\nAn American call on a share that pays no dividend should never be exercised early, so that \\(C=c\\). An American put should be exercised early when the share price is sufficiently low, so that \\(P>p\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:8.exercises#29", "source_key": "fd-textbook:8.exercises", "chapter": 8, "section": "8.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 29, "title": "Chapter 8, Option Markets and the Properties of Option Prices — 8.exercises Exercises", "body": "8.1 An investor holds two call contracts with a strike of $90 on a share. The company declares a 3-for-1 stock split. State the terms of the investor's position after the split.\nThe strike is multiplied by 1/3 and the number of shares by 3: each contract becomes a call on 300 shares with a strike of $30. The investor holds two such contracts, on 600 shares in total.\n8.2 A share trades at $40 and pays no dividend. The risk-free rate is 6 percent. Find the lower bound for the price of (a) a four-month European call with a strike of $36 and (b) a four-month European put with a strike of $44.\n(a) \\(40-36e^{-0.06\\times4/12}=40-35.287=\\$4.71\\). (b) \\(44e^{-0.02}-40=43.129-40=\\$3.13\\).\n8.3 A share trades at $22 and pays no dividend. A six-month European put with a strike of $26 is quoted at $2.50, and the risk-free rate is 5 percent. Is there an arbitrage? If so, describe it."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:8.exercises#30", "source_key": "fd-textbook:8.exercises", "chapter": 8, "section": "8.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 30, "title": "Chapter 8, Option Markets and the Properties of Option Prices — 8.exercises Exercises", "body": "Lower bound: \\(26e^{-0.025}-22=25.358-22=\\$3.36\\). The quote of $2.50 violates it. Borrow $24.50 for six months; buy the put for $2.50 and the share for $22.00. At expiration repay \\(24.50e^{0.025}=\\$25.12\\). If \\(S_T<26\\), exercise the put and receive $26.00, for a profit of $0.88; if \\(S_T\\ge26\\), sell the share for \\(S_T\\), for a profit of \\(S_T-25.12\\ge0.88\\).\n8.4 A share trades at $65. A six-month European call with a strike of $70 costs $2.85, and the risk-free rate is 4 percent. The share pays no dividend. Find the price of the six-month European put with a strike of $70.\n\\(p=c+Ke^{-rT}-S_0=2.85+70e^{-0.02}-65=2.85+68.614-65=\\$6.46\\).\n8.5 In Exercise 8.4 the put is quoted at $7.00. Set out the arbitrage and compute the profit at expiration.\nThe put is dear. Write the put for $7.00, sell the share short for $65.00, and buy the call for $2.85: net receipt \\(7.00+65.00-2.85=\\$69.15\\), invested for six months to give \\(69.15e^{0.02}=\\$70.55\\). At expiration the share is bought for $70 in either case (by exercising the call if \\(S_T>70\\), or because the put is exercised against the arbitrageur if \\(S_T\\le70\\)) and returned. Profit: \\(70.55-70.00=\\$0.55\\) per share."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:8.exercises#31", "source_key": "fd-textbook:8.exercises", "chapter": 8, "section": "8.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 31, "title": "Chapter 8, Option Markets and the Properties of Option Prices — 8.exercises Exercises", "body": "8.6 Explain why an American put on a share that pays no dividend is always worth at least its intrinsic value, whereas a European put may not be.\nThe holder of an American put can exercise at once and receive \\(K-S_0\\); the put cannot sell for less, or buying it and exercising would be an arbitrage. The holder of a European put cannot obtain \\(K\\) until expiration, and its lower bound is \\(Ke^{-rT}-S_0\\), which is below \\(K-S_0\\). When the share price is very low, the European put is worth little more than this bound, and so less than its intrinsic value.\n8.7 A share trades at $50 and will pay a dividend of $1.00 in two months. A four-month European put with a strike of $50 costs $2.90, and the risk-free rate is 6 percent for all maturities. Find the price of the four-month European call with a strike of $50.\n\\(D=1.00e^{-0.06\\times2/12}=0.990\\). \\(Ke^{-rT}=50e^{-0.02}=49.010\\). \\(c=p+S_0-D-Ke^{-rT}=2.90+50-0.990-49.010=\\$2.90\\).\n8.8 ★ A share that pays no dividend trades at $31. The risk-free rate is 8 percent. An American call with a strike of $30 and three months to expiration costs $3.00. Find the range within which the price of the American put with the same terms must lie."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:8.exercises#32", "source_key": "fd-textbook:8.exercises", "chapter": 8, "section": "8.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 32, "title": "Chapter 8, Option Markets and the Properties of Option Prices — 8.exercises Exercises", "body": "\\(S_0-K\\le C-P\\le S_0-Ke^{-rT}\\) gives \\(31-30\\le3.00-P\\le31-30e^{-0.02}\\), that is \\(1.00\\le3.00-P\\le1.594\\). Hence \\(1.41\\le P\\le2.00\\)."}]$aim_json$::jsonb, 'Financial Derivatives (MacroNations textbook)');
